**Sources.** Data: [Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, Federal Funds Effective Rate (`FEDFUNDS`), via FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/FEDFUNDS);
[U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis, PCE price index excluding food and energy (`PCEPILFE`), via FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/PCEPILFE);
[U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis, Real Gross Domestic Product (`GDPC1`), via FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/GDPC1);
[Congressional Budget Office, Real Potential Gross Domestic Product (`GDPPOT`), via FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/GDPPOT);
recession dates: [National Bureau of Economic Research, business cycle dates](https://www.nber.org/research/business-cycle-dating), as the indicator [`USREC`, via FRED](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/USREC).
The Taylor rule and short-run output, 100 × (GDPC1 / GDPPOT − 1), are our own calculations.
Prepared files: [`data/ch14/fig14-4.csv`](https://github.com/quarcs-lab/macro102-site/blob/main/data/ch14/fig14-4.csv), [`data/ch14/recessions.csv`](https://github.com/quarcs-lab/macro102-site/blob/main/data/ch14/recessions.csv)
(see the [data notes](https://github.com/quarcs-lab/macro102-site/blob/main/data/ch14/README.md)).
Chart: inspired by Fig. 14.4 in Jones (2024), [*Macroeconomics*, 6th ed.](https://wwnorton.com/books/9781324063612)

